; `cast` takes a TYPE first, and a literal is not one (2026-08-16).
;
; Found by probe while asking a different question - whether the entry
; file can be NAMED in a qualified reference the way `Mod::name` names
; a module, because MAC-HYG-8's first hole would close if it could.
; `(::helper 4)` was the third spelling tried. It checked CLEAN and
; the program answered 32 whatever argument it was given.
;
; What it was doing: `(:: e T)` in expression position is a type
; ASCRIPTION and lowers onto `cast`, so `(::helper 4)` is
; `(cast 4 helper)` - a cast of the function VALUE `helper` to the
; "type" `4`. `exprToType` answers the SILENT WILDCARD for any shape
; it cannot convert, and a wildcard unifies with everything, so
; nothing downstream objected: the cast was performed, `main` returned
; a closure address, and a function declaring `Int` handed back a
; pointer. 32 was that pointer's low byte.
;
; The wildcard stays for shapes that MIGHT be a type this pass cannot
; resolve - that is what it is for. A literal is not one of those.
;
; THE CASE IS REACHED THROUGH A MACRO, and that is not a flourish. The
; direct spellings - `(cast 4 7)`, `(cast "hello" 7)`, `(::helper 4)` -
; cannot live in this corpus, because every `.ax` file in the tree must
; also format and must parse under the tree-sitter grammar, and BOTH of
; those model `cast`'s first argument as a type and refuse a literal
; there. Which is the finding stated another way: two of the three
; implementations already knew, and the checker was the one that did
; not. A macro parameter is a type variable to the formatter and to the
; grammar, so the source is well formed for both; only the EXPANSION
; puts a literal where the type goes, and only the checker sees it.
;
; So the diagnostic lands at the invocation and carries the expansion
; frame, which is also the only place an author could act.
(data D (A))

(macro viaMacro ((viaMacro T v)
   (:: (syntax/join m T) Int)
   (fn ((syntax/join m T)) (cast v 7))))

(viaMacro D 4)

(:: main Int)

(fn (main) 0)
